      "technique": "Atomic Force Microscopy (AFM) / Nanoindentation",
      "why_useful": "Standard optical and electron microscopes cannot measure mechanical properties. An AFM is a specialized microscope that uses a tiny probe to measure local stiffness at the micro- or nano-scale, which may be what you originally had in mind."
    }
  ],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "Standard optical and electron microscopes cannot measure mechanical stiffness. To measure the bulk stiffness of a soft hydrogel, you should use a rheometer to measure shear modulus or a universal mechanical tester to measure compressive modulus. If you specifically need to map stiffness at a microscopic level, you would need an Atomic Force Microscope (AFM), which is not currently available on the equipment list."
}

